{"title":"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 1","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Video_solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Remark\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remark</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>There are 2026 integers greater than 1 written on a blackboard, not necessarily different. In a move, Confucius chooses two integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/c/1cce1bd621aff652b0983cbc94a24545e5f4bdcb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"48\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/9/d/49d98ffe996c0f0d3b82b284cba2113439fefea9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" /> from different places on the blackboard and replaces the two integers with\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/0/a/70a2f03b6ab6c46e033d068c8ab550af5d1bfec5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\gcd(m,n) \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\frac{\\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)}{\\gcd(m,n)}.\\]\" width=\"221\" height=\"43\" />\n</p><p>He continues to make moves while it is possible to do so. \n</p><p>(a). Prove that, regardless of the choices of Confucius, after finitely many moves, exactly one integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> on the blackboard is greater than 1.\n</p><p>(b). Prove that the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> does not depend on the choices of Confucius. \n</p><p>(Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/5/a75c1d5e24ac094e4b383e8c7034d2bddb95c570.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"71\" height=\"20\" /> denotes the greatest common divisor of positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/4/1940a0fbb129cd6ebaebfc3d5ba42d8082219659.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\operatorname{lcm}(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"72\" height=\"20\" /> denotes the least common multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />.)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_solution\">Video solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Video solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/D_-_SK5-bjo\">https://youtu.be/D_-_SK5-bjo</a> (by Dedekind Cuts)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Say we remove one element, call it <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a59bb1293ee3f6dec19de4019a7178874219ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />. Form the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/1/9f161c6ac6813e09f67724c3db36922924d45ac9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$45 \\times 45$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"60\" height=\"15\" /> matrix and call it our blackboard <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/2/382cea990fadd43376cf42e8e0597a5eb13a67c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{B}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" />. At each step, choose two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/7/5178b3425b473398afb0f762fed4c027ddeef9e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"67\" height=\"15\" /> such that they are replaced in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/2/382cea990fadd43376cf42e8e0597a5eb13a67c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{B}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> to \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/5/45523c72364a3e2875f5a2f87aac8e2d9e41f242.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\gcd(m,n)\\quad\\text{and}\\quad\\frac{\\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)}{\\gcd(m,n)}=\\frac{mn}{\\gcd(m,n)^2}\\]\" width=\"327\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/a/47ab6a28adc17eac7f1dde01b61e28217e8c6492.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g = \\gcd(m,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />. \n</p><p><b>Lemma 1:</b> The 2025 elements in the matrix have the property that they will never degenerate to a case where all elements are 1.\n</p><p><b>Proof of Lemma 1:</b>\n</p><p>Suppose two entries <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/7/5178b3425b473398afb0f762fed4c027ddeef9e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"67\" height=\"15\" /> are inserted. Then, they are replaced by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/1/1/311cabda3a9b09f0dde217303ca9d1cd9201dcf6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/2/b12dabaee39ffbf1e8a29ba8f1a3b9a86b1b1599.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{mn}{g^2}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"33\" height=\"38\" />. Assume that both replacements degenerate to 1. Then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/e/fee8ac1ef24a64e6d99b4e46c05a8ecabfdda486.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"41\" height=\"15\" /> from which <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/0/150c08478d63c3d2f5cc0de4222ea9183673d7ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$mn$\" width=\"26\" height=\"8\" /> is the remaining. But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/7/5178b3425b473398afb0f762fed4c027ddeef9e2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"67\" height=\"15\" /> hence this is impossible. So at least one element after this process is greater than 1.\n</p><p><b>Lemma 2</b>: Suppose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/2/382cea990fadd43376cf42e8e0597a5eb13a67c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathcal{B}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"12\" height=\"13\" /> contains <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/d/f/8df9cf1d6f6e2474e61796d7425ac78ce112893b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\geq 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" /> elements greater than 1. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/4/da48fdc12b7680253e47175c2c0009c28871b245.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" /> is the removed element, then adding back <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a59bb1293ee3f6dec19de4019a7178874219ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> and performing the same moveset as above degenerates the new blackboard to a case where exactly one element is greater than 1. \n</p><p><b>Proof of  Lemma 2:</b>\n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/4/dc4349b8593e2a218b4efc7236e4a03a8fd30581.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> be two numbers in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" />. Consider their prime factorizations. For any prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/7/3a7530cf4ed3b086da076905f18d80e509c68da6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/6/6d640038e7937fdca5f677ac4d87b0133ed943da.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" /> be the power of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/b/e9bdf3e496517e8f8742f81dd774be1f93cf6e8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" /> respectively. The moveset replaces the values with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/7/fb71a277fcf2a7295a1ad44e7d57526f515a2fb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\min(v_p(a),v_p(b))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"133\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/0/f20d3a322e203dfa2eb1464a008734854fa5d52c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|v_p(a)-v_p(b)|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"113\" height=\"20\" />. However, this is just the Euclidean algorithm on the exponents of any given prime in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/b/e9bdf3e496517e8f8742f81dd774be1f93cf6e8c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a,b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"27\" height=\"18\" />. The algorithm always ends with a nonzero value and one zero value for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/5/2f5f9b5dadda1f7956fdcb0aa41c9b39d15fca51.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p(a,b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"58\" height=\"20\" />. Hence, one of the numbers is greater than one and the other is one itself. Therefore, exactly one number exists that is greater than 1.\n</p><p><b>2026 IMO Q1 Part A</b>:\n</p><p><b>Lemma 2</b> proves the result. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>We now prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a59bb1293ee3f6dec19de4019a7178874219ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> is invariant and no matter what we choose the value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a59bb1293ee3f6dec19de4019a7178874219ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> to be in the original 2026 matrix, the result always holds. \n</p><p><b>Lemma 3:</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/3/1/e31b6c6e66feffbdf41b0e1560b0356d842a6d07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd\\bigl(v_p(a_1),v_p(a_2),\\dots,v_p(a_n)\\bigr)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -7px\" width=\"239\" height=\"24\" /> is invariant under any move.\n</p><p><b>Proof of Lemma 3:</b>\n</p><p>If Confucius chooses <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />, then let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/1/7c1a45d104f3a38285ad7cbf808b7cee9ad65fb9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x=v_p(m), y=v_p(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"166\" height=\"20\" />. These moves replace <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/2/d32cfaf8959d2d5b4b8656c5483dba489fd9e742.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m,n), \\frac{\\operatorname{lcm}(m,n)}{\\gcd(m,n)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"168\" height=\"45\" />. By Lemma 2, that is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/0/9e00b511cd96bd505ced126ec3a6fe6494b2308b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\min(x,y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"74\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/2/e526fbac5ee18e20a7293e9cb9c317989b31988a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$|x-y|$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"18\" />. In Lemma 2, we also showed this is the Euclidean Algorithm. Hence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/c/e7c847863262e3e66f5d0b56b20ac1ae62891d9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x,y)=\\gcd(\\min(x,y),|x-y|)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"270\" height=\"20\" />, which is the same greatest common factor. Extending to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> such integers, the result follows.\n</p><p><b>2026 IMO Q1 Part B</b>:\n</p><p>By <b>Lemma 2</b> exactly one number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> remains and all others are 1. Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/4/a747277e0ce7e1bd29dd94c75c2dd7de10e1e961.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(M,1,1,\\dots,1)=M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"190\" height=\"20\" />. In addition, the greatest common factor of all numbers on the blackboard is just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/6/a5643d9b46ee78e49580cfa920af530647954365.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\prod_{p} p^{v_{p}(M)} = M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -25px\" width=\"119\" height=\"45\" />. However, by <b>Lemma 3</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/e/62e046a074f35ffb6b828bce018636803340a7d6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p^{v_{p}(M)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"22\" /> for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is invariant hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" /> is invariant. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>~Pinotation\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remark\">Remark</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Remark\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>Honestly this was a really good problem, especially for a question 1. Its approach is clever, and its usage of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/2/be298c21806debb3e79cdb3ab3386c57c2562998.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"15\" height=\"12\" /> really simplify the blackboard. The fact one can just remove an element to form a perfect square too is clever, so such a great problem for the IMO.&#160;:&gt;\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><b>Part a</b>: Assume FTSOC, Confucius is able to do infinite moves. Now, if we turn a number into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/d/78db23e86da8fe319c63bdb5eaf6d3fd8fefa17b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2025$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> times, we can't do any more moves, so we can turn a number into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> at most <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/7/e5711c8d31d3bc3119aba377c9bda0f9dfb14e4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2024$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"36\" height=\"13\" /> times. Thus, there must be an infinite number of moves that don't turn a number into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, which means an infinite number of moves where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/a/f7a0289dc2cf59a8fbd19e1857afd8a7f972cc26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m, n) &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"112\" height=\"20\" />. For each of these infinite moves, the product of the numbers goes from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/0/150c08478d63c3d2f5cc0de4222ea9183673d7ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$mn$\" width=\"26\" height=\"8\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/f/d8fc498ee4eab8613f19f4212fd3984a272fc95d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{mn}{\\gcd(m, n)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"82\" height=\"39\" />. Thus, the product decreases, but it can't decrease for an infinite number of moves while staying a positive integer, so we are done. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/9/ee948e5be4abc2202e7bbb31e25748f0add452e3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" /> follows from the fact that the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/1/1312f5d8b25bc71878e98d5baa606574c23657e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(m, n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/d/3fd5e5247c42ded3ed7739a9e074e225b38321bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\text{lcm}(m, n)}{\\gcd(m. n)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"81\" height=\"45\" /> can't both be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> unless both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/0/1506fa1077cbd2bf829cdb9326e6f315beeec87a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m=n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"86\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p><b>Part b</b>: Assume FTSOC, we start with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/3/6b3c3c7c8b37696edc4ee36ddeb07eea4cefc3e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_1, a_2, ..., a_n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"94\" height=\"13\" /> and Confucius can end with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/f/79fd96bcc36d78d0fcd1183a2d0d7db1b7c145b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M=x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"56\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/6/e6614c2b894cdb27cf540532b424122f76dc2b6e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M=y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"55\" height=\"18\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/d/74ddb8309acb92903e40f1ba96475de8f7761cdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\neq y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"46\" height=\"18\" />, there must be a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/1/1/111de49b7092c1108e36124868d48dbe37df0648.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_px=a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"63\" height=\"15\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/6/1b6e8140ddd340986f89524f70fbfec80973ed41.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_py=b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"60\" height=\"19\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/8/3/3834f0cef2a1f4d8524aa5bc8e3faca298550862.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a, b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"25\" height=\"16\" /> are nonnegative distinct integers. Now, we will consider the exponents of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" />, to form a new sequence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/d/94d9565abaadf04609a2e9941aa2d20b0a299b8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"15\" />. Now, Confucius's operation turns <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> numbers, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/6851ca945f78b10a85c14fc57fbaf75e69f57241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m, n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"34\" height=\"11\" /> in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/d/94d9565abaadf04609a2e9941aa2d20b0a299b8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"15\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/2/da21546b871a54296800b0655bfd8bdcdd60e232.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m, n-m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" /> when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/5/655fd1ce35547f10b59927803e384b6b45c57d20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\geq m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"53\" height=\"16\" />. Since Confucius's operations in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/2/412787c048e28774dc63fc27db42dc52ca858de7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"14\" height=\"10\" /> end with all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/1/5d1e4485dc90c450e8c76826516c1b2ccb8fce16.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"21\" height=\"14\" />, we have that in <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/d/94d9565abaadf04609a2e9941aa2d20b0a299b8a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b_i$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"12\" height=\"15\" />, his operations and with all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s and either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />. We see that turning <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/5/6851ca945f78b10a85c14fc57fbaf75e69f57241.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m, n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"34\" height=\"11\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/2/da21546b871a54296800b0655bfd8bdcdd60e232.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m, n-m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" /> when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/5/655fd1ce35547f10b59927803e384b6b45c57d20.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\geq m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"53\" height=\"16\" /> keeps the gcd constant. Since we must end up with all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/1/bc1f9d9bf8a1b606a4188b5ce9a2af1809e27a89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />s and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/3/8/938070a1012b8c0f736c7e3357717a30420cbdfb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(0, 0, ..., 0, a)=gcd(0, 0, ..., 0, b)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"275\" height=\"20\" /> or just <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/a/b/5abd2c942ae299fd26e18d0ca5d390a7ca4a8c34.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=b$\" width=\"42\" height=\"12\" /> and we are done.\n</p><p>~Math645\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can look at every prime separately. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/3/1c39926efbcdc7d77c9337f5d48dd8e1761aec2d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{v}$\" width=\"11\" height=\"9\" /> be a list of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/a/57a8b4f300506faeb7b32095607639446614ba50.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n=2026$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"73\" height=\"14\" /> registers initialized to the powers of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> in the list on the blackboard. A move sends\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/f/84f22a787b317ceb0b09066e5f80cbda656e2562.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(v_i,v_j)\\mapsto (|v_i-v_j|, \\min(v_i, v_j))\\]\" width=\"245\" height=\"19\" />\n</p><p>This is exactly one step in the the Euclidean algorithm, which runs until the terminal state <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/f/5ff49b60eaf5cf4f98a39a39d0e71df7ae12b454.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(0, \\gcd(v_i, v_j))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"113\" height=\"20\" />. The proof the Euclidean algorithm works is the same as the proof Confucius will end up at\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/5/225bde20e676a587537332a877e36c190043b83c.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(0, 0, \\dots, \\gcd(\\mathbf{v})).\\]\" width=\"135\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>The algorithm is a semi-invariant: at least one register gets smaller with each step, and no register gets larger. There is always another step until the terminal\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/7/4/674fa5c26e637084d3606e94e754f7019d63be0a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(0, 0, \\dots, \\text{something})\\]\" width=\"151\" height=\"18\" />\n</p><p>There is also an invariant in this algorithm: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/f/18fd41e3942c149e5e6766131ff9ad83b78ece06.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(\\mathbf{v})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"55\" height=\"20\" /> remains constant at every step. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/a/c/bacc894996d0f3f1357fde786c761e1d418fd127.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(0, 0, \\dots, \\text{something}) = \\text{something}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"282\" height=\"20\" /> that terminal <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/1/a91e706a4e37442166de5f5271a4d8cf42175a88.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\text{something}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"17\" /> must be the greatest common divisor of the original registers.\n</p><p>Exponentiating from the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/2/be298c21806debb3e79cdb3ab3386c57c2562998.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$v_p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"15\" height=\"12\" /> setting to any product of primes gives the terminal\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/2/792c91ba6254efeb2f1d9417b9ee1d02b2c5c486.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[(1, 1, \\dots, \\prod_{p\\text{ prime}}p^{\\gcd(\\mathbf{v_p})})\\]\" width=\"181\" height=\"39\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_1&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO\" title=\"2026 IMO\">2026 IMO</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions,_with_authors\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors\">IMO Problems and Solutions, with authors</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Mathematics_competitions_resources\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Mathematics competitions resources\">Mathematics competitions resources</a></li></ul>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO\" title=\"2026 IMO\">2026 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b>First Question</b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_6\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 6\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2026_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2026 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\"IMO Problems and Solutions\"> All IMO Problems and Solutions</a></b>\n</td></tr></tbody></table>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261018023645\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.053 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.066 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 629/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 867/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 52/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 3/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 20900/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    6.733      1 Template:IMO_box\n100.00%    6.733      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:37205:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261018023645 and revision id 291861. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}